le bit the  hoover institution  at stanford what is it  it's a um  gathering of scholars a think tank is  what it's normally called  there are a number of nobel laureates  there including milton friedman edward  teller  it's um it's a place where  you can do research  on domestic policy issues on foreign  policy issues  with absolutely no dictated agenda  no one ever told me  what the parameters of my study would be  i proposed what i wanted to look at  which  when i originally was given a a grant a  postdoctoral grant from hoover  it was to analyze the impact of western  capital on the soviet economy  that's what i wanted to do for all i  know my conclusions might have turned  out that  it's a very  healthy  impact and and there should be more of  it the point is  hoover didn't ask me in any way to  define my um  potential conclusions it was uh it's  really an organization dedicated to  research with policy implications that  might affect the welfare of the public  hoover as at stanford in palo alto  california but you live in middlebury  virginia 54 miles from i think this  studio why how do you get how do you get  to do that well i was a research fellow  at or a research assistant to martin  anderson for about  18 months  and then i submitted with his blessing  this proposal to specifically focus on  soviet debt  and at that point  i received a two-year  fellowship from hoover  through the national fellows program  and the particular type of fellowship i  i received  the tradition was that people spent part  of the time at hoover and then they went  to washington where they were free to  affiliate with  american enterprise institute if if they  needed that or to just pursue research  the idea being that  public policy issues are often decided  in washington and some of the best  statistics resources and people involved  on a particular topic the institutions  in my case  the world bank and the imf  senate banking committee and house  foreign affairs committee the action was  in washington  so i came here to do that part of my  fellowship  and when my fellowship ended  i was well into into the book  i was still doing research in that area  still needing access to statistics to  update it  and policy things were breaking  everywhere because of the actions  gorbachev was taking  so i wanted to continue my affiliation  with hoover and they  made me a research fellow which now puts  me as  a member of the scholarly staff i have  access to the archives at hoover which  is  tremendous in the area of soviet studies  east european studies  i'm in contact with hoover scholars  two to three times a week  and i go back and forth for seminars  um at hoover when it involves this  subject live with your husband in  middlebury virginia any children no  that we have about 10 minutes this we  have to wrap it up and we've got a lot  to talk about yet i assume somebody that  if they want more than i've gotten by  this book which at  2250 probably wouldn't uh be unpleasant  to you um  there's a chapter called proper  perspective once again the title of the  book the coming soviet christ  gorbachev's desperate pursuit of credit  in western financial markets  because we only have 10 minutes i'm  gonna have to go through these quickly  but you have a number of points in this  last chapter that i want you to briefly  explain number one don't lose track of  moscow's fundamental problem  there i think we need to focus on this  massive budget deficit which means they  don't have the internal resources to  fund the military  investment and consumer spending as much  as they want so we have to interpret  all political initiatives by gorbachev  in terms of what must be his fundamental  problem one to which he's admitted  two don't underestimate the importance  of trading with the west for the soviet  union  there's a tendency for businessmen in  the west to downplay  the importance of trade with the soviets  because then they can say it doesn't  matter we can't affect the soviet  economy so why don't we just go ahead  and do business  and the point i want to make is it  matters a great deal soviet government  is effectively the agent  they can bring in goods from the west  and sell them for a tremendous profit it  helps plug up a hole  in their financial condition  it's also the only way out  of their economic conundrum right now  they need goods imported from the west  to get workers to be motivated to work  more productively they need the  technology from the west to  improve the productivity of their  equipment  it's just vital for them to have this  trade with the west it's not something  to be dismissed three don't be  excessively flattered by soviet moves  toward capitalism  yeah this is  this is a psychological problem  we start telling ourselves that the  soviets have finally seen the light and  they're moving to our way of thinking  we have to remind ourselves that what  gorbachev is doing is not capitalism  he's just taking a few expedient  elements of capitalism again the  harshest elements bankruptcy and  unemployment and failure  and using that because he's in an  emergency situation at home  four don't promote a personality cult  for gorbachev  this i think  everyone's aware of it's it's easy to do  we focus on this  personality and we have this tendency to  want to  deal with the man when we should be  dealing with the system  we have to tell ourselves that  gorbachev he could be replaced tomorrow  by  the soviet minister of defense or the  head of the kgb  and we don't want to suddenly have our  whole policy toward the soviet union  disrupted because they've changed people  at the top  um i think it's a big mistake to focus  on  personalities and could just have  terrible  implications for our overall strategy  toward the soviets five don't trade  economic and financial assistance for  soviet political concessions  this is a very tough one  because  in the united states especially  it bothers us when people aren't allowed  to immigrate  it bothers us when dissidents are thrown  into prison and we have this desire to  do whatever we can  to um to help them  and so as embodied in for example the  jacksonvanic amendment  there is this  quid pro quo that if the soviets start  complying with what we consider  standards of basic human decency  we will give them most favored nation  trade status  and allow them eligibility for  government-backed trade credits  that i think is a  not just an inappropriate trade-off but  a crass  situation to be talking about human  rights life and death on one hand and  financial favors on the other so i i  would move away from that linkage  six don't get confused over labels it's  all a transfer of capital  well this just focuses on the monitoring  problem um capital is now flowing to the  soviet union through joint ventures  because equity capital is not so much  different from debt and yet it doesn't  get picked up in the debt statistics  it's still a transfer of western  financial resources  there are the euro bond offerings that  the soviets now started and  those aren't picked up by the bis  figures or the oecd  so i think we need a new online  monitoring system just to keep track of  our total exposure to the soviet union  financially  seven improved monitoring capabilities  same idea  eight the united states must lead  again  this is the tough  part of the policy issue  i'm convinced that right now our  our allies are working on james baker  to get him to go along with this let's  help gorbachev approach  i think the united states is going to  have to  make its position clear to our allies  that  if our goal is to get the soviets to  transfer resources  out of the military  and into the consumption sector  the last thing we want to do  is to relieve that pressure that  internal economic pressure to make that  change  by providing the consumer goods from the  outside with our own money  in that sense we're giving a reprieve to  the kremlin  and we're just stopping this transition  process that i think would any soviet  leader not just gorbachev would be  compelled to respond to  and so we don't want to we don't want to  reduce that pressure we want them to  have to make that  decision of  butter versus guns on their own  are you at all surprised by any other  aspect of writing a book any reaction  you got was it difficult do you hope to  do it again  the book was a terrific  exercise you can't say things loosely  you verify all of your facts and figures  you think through your conclusions  and you decide what you're willing to go  to bat for  the book is getting more attention  than i would have ever hoped for but  it's it's very gratifying that it is i  think this is a  profound issue for uh every every  american taxpayer  he's the guy paying the bill for defense  he he has to be concerned about policy  that might end up with his dollars  effectively subsidizing loans to the  soviet union when did you know and what  aspect of this book first got people's  attention when did you know that that  this was going to get reviewed and it  was going to make a difference  you know the um  the soviets have have done the best pr  for me  because of  as the book was going to press and then  the copies were in galley form  that's when they first made the  announcement  that a budget deficit existed for 40  years they've been saying it's a surplus  so there i am going on record in prince  saying it can't be it's got to be a  terrific deficit and here's why  well to have  my assertions confirmed to the supreme  soviet by the the finance minister was a  boon and i think people thought maybe  there's other things in there that are  worth looking at did they do it because  of you  um  i'm sure it would be delusions of  grandeur for me to think so um  but  a review copy was made available in  early december to task they demanded it  in a typewritten request to my publisher  to see a copy of the book  and i think he tried to hedge a bit  and they demanded again more strongly a  week later  i i don't think it's just me there are a  lot of other people now who are saying  that the soviets are running a deficit  and it may be the pressure was such they  felt they should come clean even if at  first they just admitted a little and a  little and now they're saying no three  times as much as we said at first  i think they're responding to pressure  from me and others  in the western community of  sovietologists especially people who  look at the financial points responding  to that  this is what the book looks like the  title of it is the coming soviet crash  and our guest for the last hour  has been dr judy shelton who is a  native of los angeles currently lives in  middleburg virginia  has a phd in finance from the university  of utah a master's from the same school  and a bachelor in business  administration from portland state  university also has studied at ucla and  is currently a research fellow at the  hoover institution at stanford  university thank you for your time my  pleasure thank you for having me  [Music]  you've been watching an interview with  author judy shelton  for more information you may write to  her publisher the free press at 866  third avenue new york new york  one zero zero two two  and in a moment on c-span a look ahead  to the upcoming week in congress  [Music]  [Laughter]
 

